---
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import comparison from "../../data/comparison.json";
import { sites, money, pct, constraints } from "../../lib/model";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

const SIDS = ["S1", "S2", "S3", "S4", "S5"] as const;
type Sid = (typeof SIDS)[number];

interface Step {
  spend: number;
  storage_kl: number;
  water_closure: number;
  garden_water_satisfaction: number;
  imported_kl: number;
  overflow_kl: number;
  cost_total: number;
  headroom: number;
  fits: boolean;
}

const short: Record<string, string> = Object.fromEntries(sites.map((s) => [s.id, s.short]));
const rows = comparison.rows as Record<Sid, Step[]>;
const sum = comparison.summary as Record<Sid, { saturates_at: number; largest_affordable_spend: number }>;
const at = (sid: Sid, spend: number): Step => rows[sid].find((r) => r.spend === spend)!;
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Worked comparison: what does another dollar of storage buy?"
  description="One design idea taken through all five sites. The same thousand dollars of rainwater storage does five different things, and at three sites it does nothing at all."
  wide
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p>{comparison.question}</p>

  <p>
    It is worth working through because the intuition is so strong and so often
    wrong. A site that imports water is short of water; a tank holds water;
    therefore a bigger tank fixes it. That reasoning is correct at exactly one of
    these five sites.
  </p>

  <h2>Method</h2>
  <p>{comparison.method}</p>
  <p>
    Everything else in the excellent reference design is held fixed. The only
    variable is the money spent on tank shells, and the only outputs that move are
    the ones a larger tank can move.
  </p>

  <h2>The result</h2>

  <Figure
    caption="Water closure at the excellent reference design as new-storage spend rises. The reference design sits at $4,000. Rows past each site's affordable limit are shown so the budget wall is visible, not hidden."
    basis="model estimate"
  >
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Storage spend</th>
          {SIDS.map((s) => <th scope="col">{short[s]}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          comparison.steps.map((spend) => (
            <tr class={spend === comparison.base_spend ? "base" : ""}>
              <th scope="row">
                {money(spend)}{spend === comparison.base_spend && <span class="tag">reference</span>}
              </th>
              {
                SIDS.map((s) => {
                  const r = at(s, spend);
                  return (
                    <td class={r.fits ? "" : "over"}>
                      {pct(r.water_closure)}
                      <span class="sub">{r.storage_kl} kL{!r.fits && " · over budget"}</span>
                    </td>
                  );
                })
              }
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <h2>Five sites, five different answers</h2>

  <h3>Brisbane: storage is the right purchase, and it has a knee</h3>
  <p>
    The townhouse is the one site where the intuition holds. Going from{" "}
    {money(2000)} to {money(5000)} lifts water closure from{" "}
    {pct(at("S3", 2000).water_closure)} to {pct(at("S3", 5000).water_closure)},
    cuts imports from {at("S3", 2000).imported_kl} kL to {at("S3", 5000).imported_kl} kL,
    and eliminates overflow entirely. Brisbane's roof out-collects its household over
    the year, so its shortfall really is a timing problem, and timing problems are
    what tanks solve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then it stops. Above {money(sum.S3.saturates_at)} the curve is flat: there is no
    more surplus left to catch. <strong>Spending past that point buys kilolitres that
    are never full.</strong>
  </p>

  <h3>Canberra, Alice Springs and Adelaide: storage buys nothing</h3>
  <p>
    At three sites the water closure column does not move at all across the entire
    range — {money(2000)} to {money(10000)}, and at Canberra that is 20 kL against
    82 kL of tank, for an identical {pct(at("S1", 10000).water_closure)}.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reason is that these sites are <strong>supply-limited, not storage-limited</strong>.
    Canberra's roof yields about 142 kL a year and the household alone wants more
    than that once the garden is ignored entirely. Adelaide collects 92 kL against a
    219 kL domestic demand. Alice Springs collects 73 kL and its bore is capped at
    100 kL. A tank cannot hold water that never fell on the roof, and every one of
    these sites empties its tank every year no matter how large it is.
  </p>
  <p>
    What would work at these three is different in each case: more catchment area,
    lower domestic demand, or — at Adelaide, where rain and demand are out of phase
    by six months — accepting that some imports are structural and designing the
    garden around them.
  </p>

  <h3>Darwin: storage keeps paying, and the budget stops you</h3>
  <p>
    Darwin is the only site where the curve is still climbing at {money(10000)}:
    water closure rises from {pct(at("S5", 2000).water_closure)} to{" "}
    {pct(at("S5", 10000).water_closure)}, and garden water satisfaction from{" "}
    {pct(at("S5", 2000).garden_water_satisfaction)} to{" "}
    {pct(at("S5", 10000).garden_water_satisfaction)}. With 411 kL landing on the roof
    there is always more to catch.
  </p>
  <p>
    But look at the overflow column: even at 122 kL of tank, Darwin still throws away{" "}
    {at("S5", 10000).overflow_kl} kL and still imports {at("S5", 10000).imported_kl} kL.
    And the budget runs out first — {money(sum.S5.largest_affordable_spend)} is the most
    Darwin can afford before the total passes {money(constraints.budget_aud)}.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Darwin's problem is not solvable by storage at this budget</strong>, and a design
    that spends its whole allowance trying is worse than one that spends a third of it
    and puts the rest into demand reduction.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="What the step from the reference design to the largest affordable spend actually buys at each site."
    basis="model estimate"
  >
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Site</th>
          <th scope="col">Largest affordable spend</th>
          <th scope="col">Extra cost from the reference</th>
          <th scope="col">Water closure gained</th>
          <th scope="col">Garden gained</th>
          <th scope="col">Verdict</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          SIDS.map((s) => {
            const best = at(s, sum[s].largest_affordable_spend);
            const base = at(s, comparison.base_spend);
            const dw = best.water_closure - base.water_closure;
            const dg = best.garden_water_satisfaction - base.garden_water_satisfaction;
            return (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{short[s]}</th>
                <td>{money(sum[s].largest_affordable_spend)}</td>
                <td>{money(best.cost_total - base.cost_total)}</td>
                <td>{dw > 0 ? `+${Math.round(dw * 100)} pp` : "none"}</td>
                <td>{dg > 0 ? `+${Math.round(dg * 100)} pp` : "none"}</td>
                <td class="verdict">
                  {s === "S3" && "Buy it, up to the knee"}
                  {s === "S5" && "Buy some; the budget binds before the benefit does"}
                  {(s === "S1" || s === "S2" || s === "S4") && "Do not buy it; spend elsewhere"}
                </td>
              </tr>
            );
          })
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Figure>

  <h2>Why this comparison is on the site at all</h2>
  <p>
    Because the course got it wrong. The reference designs used to spend{" "}
    {money(9000)} on storage at every site, which is past the knee at four of the
    five and unaffordable at all of them once establishment, first flush, treatment,
    irrigation and contingency were priced. Costed against the full schedule, those
    designs were {money(3704)} to {money(9545)} over the allowance.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fix was to spend where the curve is still rising and stop where it is not,
    which is the same judgement this page is asking you to make. The bands on the{" "}
    <a href={withBase("/method/")}>method page</a> were recomputed afterwards.
  </p>

  <h3>What to take from it</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Find your own curve before you spend.</strong> Step your tank size and plot the
      indicator against capacity. The shape tells you whether your site is
      storage-limited or supply-limited, and they need opposite responses.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A flat curve is information, not a failure.</strong> Three of these sites say
      plainly that the money belongs somewhere else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Watch both water indicators.</strong> At Darwin, storage moves garden water
      satisfaction more than it moves water closure. A site can be getting
      meaningfully better at something the headline number barely registers.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  th[scope="row"] {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 500;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  td {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .sub {
    display: block;
    color: var(--at-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
  }

  .over {
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .base th,
  .base td {
    background: var(--at-accent-soft);
  }

  .tag {
    display: block;
    color: var(--at-primary);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .verdict {
    font-variant-numeric: normal;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
